disorder during the third trimester of pregnancy?',NULL,'B','Of the given options, lithium is the only acceptable one in terms of both safety and efficacy during
pregnancy. However, it is contraindicated in breast feeding woman because reports suggest that
there is low renal clearance of lithium in infants resulting in toxic levels of lithium. This may result
in life-treathening infantile complications. 
Reportedly, lithium use during the first trimester of pregnancy has been associated with
cardiovascular anomalies (e.g. Epstein’s anomaly), midfacial defects, and other defects;
nonetheless, the benefits to the mother substantially outweigh the risks of fetal anomaly. However,
if a pregnant woman has been exposed to lithium during the first trimester, an ultrasound and
echocardiogram should be performed at 16-20 weeks gestation to exclude fetal anomalies,
especially cardiac ones. Lithium is even safer during the second and third trimesters when
cardiogensis has already occurred and the risk of fetal malformations is significantly decreased. If
lithium has been tapered during the first trimester or before the conception, it can be safely
resumed in second or third trimester if indicated.
Lithium requirements increase in the third trimester; however, dose reduction by 25% is
recommended before delivery to prevent possible neonatal toxicity presenting with floppy baby
syndrome (hypotonia, cyanosis, poor suckling). The dose should then be increased after delivery.
(Option A) Carbamazepine has been shown poor efficacy in controlling an acute manic episode in
bipolar disorder.
(Options C and E) Clozapine or mirtazapine is not indicated or useful for treatment of an acute
manic episode in bipolar disorder.
(Option D) Sodium valproate has significant teratogenic effect and should not be used during
pregnancy.
NOTE - A recent meta-analysis has confirmed that the most efficacious acute treatments for mania
are the antipsychotics rather than the traditional ‘mood stabilizers’ lithium, valproate and
carbamazepine. Specifically, that report found that the preferred options – after taking into account
both efficacy and tolerability – were risperidone, olanzapine and haloperidol.',NULL,'Retain the source-era answer for question-bank study; verify current Australian guidance before clinical use.',NULL,NULL,'2023','v2.9.4',1,'text','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-6c39718a3400","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"298dfab62b52f6a35cb4","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_page":1542,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"native","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high') ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET source_id=excluded.source_id,source_question_no=excluded.source_question_no,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,system=excluded.system,topic=excluded.topic,clinical_task=excluded.clinical_task,stem_en=excluded.stem_en,answer_key=excluded.answer_key,source_explanation_en=excluded.source_explanation_en,memory_rule_en=excluded.memory_rule_en,image_ref=excluded.image_ref,source_year=excluded.source_year,import_version=excluded.import_version,active=1,options_mode=excluded.options_mode,provenance_json=excluded.provenance_json,current_answer=excluded.current_answer,current_answer_status=excluded.current_answer_status,current_evidence_json=excluded.current_evidence_json,currentness_risk=excluded.currentness_risk;
