eye is worse than 6/9.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-c4a3347019ca","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"ad88b17a5c12c9089ebe","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_page":1668,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"native","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high','{"record_id":"ad88b17a5c12c9089ebe","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_year":2023,"source_page":1668,"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"A 65-year-old man presented to the emergency department with complaint of chest pain that turned out to be of cardiac origin. Electrocardiographic changes were consistent with inferior ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI). He\nunderwent coronary artery bypass graft the next day. He is a commercial driver and on discharge wants to know if he can continue driving. Which one of the following would be the most appropriate advice?","options":{"A":"He can drive private and commercial vehicles as long as he feels fine.","B":"He should write a letter to road safety department.","C":"No commercial driving for four weeks.","D":"No commercial driving for three months","E":"He can never drive commercial vehicles, but he can drive private vehicles after three months."},"source_answer":"D","source_explanation":"After coronary artery bypass grafting, one should not drive private vehicles for at least four weeks and commercial vehicles for three months.\nTOPIC REVIEW \nThere are several conditions or procedures which lead to reduced ability to drive. Under these circumstances, the treating physician should give appropri